  "statement": "Two circles $x^{2}+y^{2}+2g_{1}x+2f_{1}y+c_{1}=0$ and $x^{2}+y^{2}+2g_{2}x+2f_{2}y+c_{2}=0$ are orthogonal if the tangents at a common point are perpendicular. Show that they are orthogonal if and only if $2g_{1}g_{2}+2f_{1}f_{2}=c_{1}+c_{2}$.",
  "solution": "Their centres are $C_{1}=(-g_{1},-f_{1})$ and $C_{2}=(-g_{2},-f_{2})$, with radii squared $r_{1}^{2}=g_{1}^{2}+f_{1}^{2}-c_{1}$ and $r_{2}^{2}=g_{2}^{2}+f_{2}^{2}-c_{2}$. At an intersection point $P$, orthogonality means the radii $C_{1}P$ and $C_{2}P$ are perpendicular, so by Pythagoras on triangle $C_{1}PC_{2}$ we have $|C_{1}C_{2}|^{2}=r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}$. Now $|C_{1}C_{2}|^{2}=(g_{1}-g_{2})^{2}+(f_{1}-f_{2})^{2}$. Equating: $(g_{1}-g_{2})^{2}+(f_{1}-f_{2})^{2}=(g_{1}^{2}+f_{1}^{2}-c_{1})+(g_{2}^{2}+f_{2}^{2}-c_{2})$. Expanding the left gives $g_{1}^{2}+f_{1}^{2}+g_{2}^{2}+f_{2}^{2}-2g_{1}g_{2}-2f_{1}f_{2}$; cancelling the squared terms from both sides leaves $-2g_{1}g_{2}-2f_{1}f_{2}=-c_{1}-c_{2}$, i.e. $2g_{1}g_{2}+2f_{1}f_{2}=c_{1}+c_{2}$.",
  "solutionStatus": "full",
  "hints": [
    "Write down the centres and radii of the two circles.",
    "Use the right-angle condition at P to relate distances.",
    "Expand and cancel to obtain the condition."
  ],
